BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Afternoon Lecture Series: Race\, Gender\, and the Salem Witch Tria
 ls
DTSTART:20261030T120000
DTEND:20261030T140000
DTSTAMP:20261027T170709Z
UID:3ed3f4fe1bed116fabf591296ddeb1@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Beyond the Trials: Race\, Gender\, and Power in 1692 The Salem
  Witch Trials are often remembered as a story of fear and hysteria—but t
 he events of 1692 were also shaped by race\, gender\, social status\, and 
 power. Who was accused\, who was believed\, and whose voices were heard? J
 oin Dr. Elizabeth Matelski for a thought-provoking look at the Salem Witch
  Trials through the lens of race and gender. Explore the lesser-known dyna
 mics of prejudice\, inequality\, and power that influenced the events of 1
 692 and gain a deeper understanding of the people and communities behind o
 ne of the most infamous chapters in American history. Visiting Salem this 
 fall? Go beyond the familiar Salem Witch Trials sites and discover another
  North Shore connection to the story. After the lecture\, be sure to explo
 re Wenham Museum’s galleries and collections\, then step inside the 1662
  Claflin-Gerrish-Richards House\, one of the North Shore’s earliest hist
 oric dwellings. The house has a direct connection to the events of 1692. I
 n November of that year\, Wenham teenager Mary Herrick met there with Rev.
  Joseph Gerrish and Rev. John Hale\, where she made accusations involving 
 Hale’s wife and shared claims concerning the recently executed Mary East
 y. The encounter helped change Hale’s view of the witchcraft proceedings
 . It’s a chance to experience the history where it happened and see a si
 de of the Salem Witch Trials story that extends beyond Salem itself. The l
 ecture is included with general museum admission\, so make a day of your v
 isit and explore the museum before or after the program. Dr. Elizabeth Mat
 elski\, Ph.D.\, is Distinguished Professor of History at Endicott College 
 in Beverly\, Massachusetts\, where she teaches courses on race\, gender\, 
 and sexuality in American history. She earned her A.B. from Ripon College 
 and her Ph.D. from Loyola University Chicago. Matelski is a former directo
 r of two National Endowment for the Humanities (NEH) summer institutes foc
 used on the Salem Witch Trials. Her current research examines the life of 
 Robin Mingo\, a formerly enslaved Black man who lived in Beverly in the ea
 rly 1700s. This makes the museum itself part of the Salem-history experien
 ce\, rather than making the lecture feel like a standalone event. Adult Ev
 ent | Included with museum admission\, no additional cost\n\nSources: Nort
 h of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event/afternoon-lecture
 -series-race-gender-and-the-salem-witch-trials/
LOCATION:Wenham Museum\, 132 Main St.\, Route 1A\, Wenham\, MA\, 01984
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/afternoon-lecture-series-race-gender-a
 nd-the-salem-witch-trials/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Alexander's Jazz Time Band
DTSTART:20261030T000000
DTEND:20261030T000000
DTSTAMP:20261027T170709Z
UID:7df8fc6279ce3f8534c43260133d11@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Alexander's Jazz Time Band\, an 11-piece 1920's-style syncopat
 ed orchestra\, will present a concert of authentic Jazz Age dance-band arr
 angements on Friday\, October 30th at The First Parish of Bolton (673 Main
  Street\, Bolton MA). The show starts at 7:00 PM and ends around 8:30. The
  program will feature\, among other things\, three early compositions by j
 azz maverick Fud Livingston\, two newly resurrected arrangements from the 
 library of the Roger Wolfe Kahn Orchestra\, and an arrangement of "Someday
  Sweetheart" written in 1931 by a young Glenn Miller. Most of the pieces o
 n the program are suitable for foxtrot dancing\; floor space will be avail
 able for that purpose throughout the show. The performance will be held in
  Davis Hall. Parking is behind the church\; entry is via the footbridge in
  back. Additional parking may be found on the west side of the main church
  building. Davis Hall is wheelchair accessible. No tickets or reservations
  are necessary. Admission is free\, but donations are gratefully accepted.
 \n\nSources: Discover Central Massachusetts\nEvent page: https://www.disco
 vercentralma.org/event/alexanders-jazz-time-band/31168/
LOCATION:The First Parish of Bolton\, The First Parish of Bolton 673 Main 
 Street\, Bolton\, MA\, 01740
URL:https://www.discovercentralma.org/event/alexanders-jazz-time-band/3116
 8/
END:VEVENT
