BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Artful Conversations: John Sloan’s “Scavenger Rocks”
DTSTART:20261105T123000
DTEND:20261105T133000
DTSTAMP:20261027T170709Z
UID:2d9751dbee3b02b35498a7b525632b@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Join Us Mid-Day for Artful Conversations Take a midday pause a
 nd experience one work of art in depth. Artful Conversations are informal
 \, docent-led gallery experiences that invite visitors to slow down\, look
  closely\, and engage in thoughtful conversation. Each session features a 
 work of art or object that is new to view—whether a recent acquisition o
 r a work newly installed in the galleries. The program begins in the Educa
 tion Room at our Downtown Campus with a brief introduction and visual cont
 ext before moving into the galleries for an extended exploration of the fe
 atured work. Guided by observation\, curiosity\, and conversation\, partic
 ipants are encouraged to share ideas\, consider different perspectives\, a
 nd discover new connections together. This week for Artful Conversations T
 he program will offer an in‑depth look at John Sloan’s Scavenger Rocks
 \, a recent acquisition that deepens our understanding of the artist’s l
 ong-standing ties to East Gloucester. The presentation begins with a brief
  overview of Sloan’s stays at the Red Cottage from 1914 to 1918\, a form
 ative period that shaped many of his most compelling Cape Ann paintings. F
 rom there\, the talk turns to the granite outcropping near Niles Beach\, d
 rawing on contemporary photographic documentation to illuminate the site
 ’s distinctive forms and its role in Sloan’s artistic process\, offeri
 ng a vivid\, tangible link between the artwork and the landscape that insp
 ired it. Dan Duffy and his wife Helene have been Innkeepers at the Beech T
 ree B&B in Rockport for 16 years. In this capacity\, he takes pride in ser
 ving as an ambassador of goodwill for travelers from around the world. He 
 is passionate about showcasing our region’s rich history\, geographical 
 uniqueness\, and artistic treasurers. As a Cape Ann Museum docent\, Dan us
 es his experience connecting with visitors while deepening his own knowled
 ge of the Museum and of the region’s vibrant social and cultural history
 .\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event
 /artful-conversations-john-sloans-scavenger-rocks/2026-11-05/
LOCATION:Cape Ann Museum\, 27 Pleasant Street\, Gloucester\, 01930
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/artful-conversations-john-sloans-scave
 nger-rocks/2026-11-05/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Clark Art Institute\, a Flim
DTSTART:20261105T180000
DTEND:20261105T190000
DTSTAMP:20261027T170709Z
UID:376307368b3b2a42f4e309e45360bc@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:This November\, the Clark hosts a series of silent slapstick c
 omedy films that highlight quick wit\, precarious situations\, and hilario
 us mishaps. The series concludes with Steamboat Bill\, Jr. (1928). The son
  of a steamboat owner (Steamboat Bill) develops a relationship with the da
 ughter of another steamboat owner\, who also happens to be Steamboat Bill
 ’s rival. When the two owners learn of their relationship\, they attempt
  to break them up. Buster Keaton’s beginnings in the rough-and-tumble wo
 rld of vaudeville prepared him to perform stunts that are too dangerous to
  be replicated today. Steamboat Bill\, Jr. contains the most iconic of the
 se\, featuring a tumbling house. The film also inspired the first Mickey M
 ouse cartoon\, all while finding comedy in the tragic Great Flood of 1927.
  (Run time: 1 hour 15 minutes) A talkback with Will Schmenner\, film schol
 ar and head of public programs\, follows the screening . Free. No registra
 tion required.\n\nSources: Explore Western Mass\nEvent page: https://explo
 rewesternmass.com/event/clark-art-institute-a-flim/
LOCATION:Clark Art Institute\, 225 South Street\, Williamstown\, MA\, 0126
 7
URL:https://explorewesternmass.com/event/clark-art-institute-a-flim/
END:VEVENT
