BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Artful Conversations: “Baptism\,” by Anne Rearick
DTSTART:20261119T123000
DTEND:20261119T133000
DTSTAMP:20261027T170709Z
UID:320156bf2c07f17c2959c2b9680b1d@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Join Us Mid-Day for Artful Conversations Take a midday pause a
 nd experience one work of art in depth. Artful Conversations are informal
 \, docent-led gallery experiences that invite visitors to slow down\, look
  closely\, and engage in thoughtful conversation. Each session features a 
 work of art or object that is new to view—whether a recent acquisition o
 r a work newly installed in the galleries. The program begins in the Educa
 tion Room at our Downtown Campus with a brief introduction and visual cont
 ext before moving into the galleries for an extended exploration of the fe
 atured work. Guided by observation\, curiosity\, and conversation\, partic
 ipants are encouraged to share ideas\, consider different perspectives\, a
 nd discover new connections together. This week for Artful Conversations T
 his presentation explores Baptism by photographer Anne Rearick\, whose wor
 k is featured in a special museum exhibition. Rooted in the tradition of d
 ocumentary photography\, Rearick distinguishes herself through her long-te
 rm relationships with the people and places she photographs\, including th
 e Gloucester community. Through Baptism and related works\, she captures t
 he importance of shared public spaces as places where people gather\, buil
 d connections\, create traditions\, and foster a sense of belonging. Her p
 hotographs reveal how community life unfolds over time\, preserving collec
 tive experiences and cultural memory. Although Miriam Weinstein has lived 
 on Cape Ann for more than three decades\, she loves finding new places and
  new stories and sharing them with visitors. She was a painting major in c
 ollege and thought of herself as a visual person until she became\, first
 \, a documentary filmmaker and then a journalist. Having spent the bulk of
  her professional life as a writer\, she now thinks she might be a word pe
 rson after all. She was a staff member at North Shore Weeklies newspapers 
 for many years. Her book\, Yiddish: A Nation of Words\, won the National J
 ewish Book award. Her book\, The Surprising Power of Family Meals gave her
  a platform to lecture and consult on the topic internationally. Miriam li
 kes changing gears\, which fits well with CAM’s varied collection. She g
 ives tours to adults\, family groups\, and kids. She enjoys moving quickly
  and making sure that everyone stays engaged. She loves the fact that tour
 s can be very personalized. Each tour is different\, depending on the inte
 rests of the docent and the guests\, as well as the changing exhibits. Muc
 h like Cape Ann\, Museum visits are creative\, personable\, idiosyncratic
 \, and rich in beauty and history.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page
 : https://northofboston.org/event/artful-conversations-baptism-by-anne-rea
 rick/2026-11-19/
LOCATION:Cape Ann Museum\, 27 Pleasant Street\, Gloucester\, 01930
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/artful-conversations-baptism-by-anne-r
 earick/2026-11-19/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Holidays at Highfield &#8211\; ’Twas the Night Before Christmas:
  Life Above & Below Stairs
DTSTART:20261119T000000
DTEND:20261220T000000
DTSTAMP:20261027T170709Z
UID:ba42d2cf6cc580b31eedc766645f5f@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Celebrating 20 years of holiday magic at Highfield Hall & Gard
 ens. This year’s Holidays at Highfield exhibition\, ’Twas the Night Be
 fore Christmas: Life Above & Below Stairs \, invites visitors to experienc
 e the final hours before Christmas morning in a grand Gilded Age mansion. 
 As the Beebe family eagerly awaits the holiday ‘above stairs’\, the se
 rvants ‘below stairs’ work tirelessly behind the scenes to prepare for
  the festivities ahead. Filled with festive beauty\, historic charm\, and 
 delightful discoveries\, the exhibition offers an immersive holiday experi
 ence for visitors of all ages. The beloved Gift Gallery returns with handc
 rafted artisan gifts\, while the holiday craft room provides hands-on fun 
 for children and families. Gather family and friends and celebrate the sea
 son with one of Cape Cod’s most beloved holiday traditions. Register onl
 ine today or call 508-495-1878 ext. 2.\n\nSources: Cape Cod Chamber\nEvent
  page: https://www.capecodchamber.org/event/holidays-at-highfield-%26%2382
 11%3b-%e2%80%99twas-the-night-before-christmas%3a-life-above-%26-below-sta
 irs/50404/
LOCATION:Highfield Hall & Gardens\, 56 Highfield Drive\, Falmouth\, MA\, 0
 2540
URL:https://www.capecodchamber.org/event/holidays-at-highfield-%26%238211%
 3b-%e2%80%99twas-the-night-before-christmas%3a-life-above-%26-below-stairs
 /50404/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Homeschool Scholars: Seasonal Rhythms
DTSTART:20261119T090000
DTEND:20261119T120000
DTSTAMP:20261027T170709Z
UID:1f915f6a3d12073d58af00232bd84a@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Join us in October for weekly homeschool programs exploring th
 e seasonal rhythms of the trails\, woodlands\, farm fields\, and coastline
 s of the Crane Estate and Appleton Farms! October is a great time to see h
 ow nature is changing and getting ready for winter. Participants will expl
 ore the coastline\, trails\, and woodlands of The Crane Estate & Appleton 
 Farms\, while making observations\, taking measurements\, and learning how
  scientists study nature as the seasons change. This is a drop-off program
  that is open to ages 5-11 and will include small group work specialized a
 ctivities based on age and peer mentoring opportunities between age groups
 . The class takes place rain or shine\, with as much time spent outdoors a
 s possible. Students should arrive dressed for the weather and bring a lun
 ch and water bottle. Homeschool Scholars: Seasonal Rhythms- This 4-class s
 eries program takes place on Thursdays from 9am-12pm on October 1\, 8\, 15
 \, & 22. Crane Estate: October 1 & 15 Appleton Farms: October 8 & 22 Tuiti
 on for the 4 classes is $275 for Trustees Members and $325 for nonmembers.
  Registration covers all listed session dates.\n\nSources: North of Boston
 \nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event/homeschool-scholars-seasonal
 -rhythms/2026-11-19/
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/homeschool-scholars-seasonal-rhythms/2
 026-11-19/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Homeschool Scholars: Stories of the Farm & Coast
DTSTART:20261119T090000
DTEND:20261119T120000
DTSTAMP:20261027T170709Z
UID:d1195b2111d3b0754bf607b7e16350@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Join us in October & November for weekly homeschool programs e
 xploring the stories of The Crane Estate & Appleton Farms. Participants wi
 ll learn the stories of the many people & animals who have lived here over
  the years from Indigenous farmers & fishers\, to European colonists\, to 
 local & invasive plant & animal species. Through inquiry and hands-on inve
 stigation we will learn what makes the Crane Estate & Appleton Farms speci
 al to their many residents & visitors\, including you! This is a drop-off 
 program that is open to ages 5-11 and will include small group work specia
 lized activities based on age and peer mentoring opportunities between age
  groups. The class takes place rain or shine\, with as much time spent out
 doors as possible. Students should arrive dressed for the weather and brin
 g a lunch and water bottle. Homeschool Scholars: Stories of the Farm & Coa
 st- This 4-class series program takes place on Thursday mornings from 9am-
 12pm on October 29\, November 5\, 12\, & 19. Crane Estate: October 29 & No
 vember 12 Appleton Farms: November 5 & 19\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEve
 nt page: https://northofboston.org/event/homeschool-scholars-stories-of-th
 e-farm-coast/2026-11-19/
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/homeschool-scholars-stories-of-the-far
 m-coast/2026-11-19/
