DTEND:20261119T133000
DTSTAMP:20261027T170709Z
UID:41208dbe2c1ddbd7c36b48020d4968@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:The Springfield Museums’ lecture series Museums à la Carte 
 celebrates 47 years of curiosity and learning. Beginning in 1979 as “Art
  à la Carte” with just a few dozen attendees\, this popular program has
  reached thousands of people over the years and expanded its reach beyond 
 the walls of the Museums when it began offering lectures digitally via Zoo
 m in 2020. Oct 29\, 12:15 pm – 1:30 pm Explorations in Abstraction Museu
 ms a la Carte Lecture $4 Nov 5\, 12:15 pm – 1:30 pm Ninety Years Later: 
 The Connecticut River Valley Flood of 1936 and the Legacy of Flood Protect
 ion Museums a la Carte Lecture $4 Nov 12\, 12:15 pm – 1:30 pm “A Few R
 ods North of the Court-House”: Mapping Revolutionary Springfield Museums
  a la Carte Lecture $4 Dec 3\, 12:15 pm – 1:30 pm The Painted Life of Gr
 egory Gillespie Join us in-person or online! Lectures continue each Thursd
 ay at 12:15 pm. In-person tickets are available at the Museums’ Welcome 
 Center on the day of the lecture\; advance virtual registration at Springf
 ieldMuseums.org/programs . Museum admission not required. For more informa
 tion\, call 413.263.6800\, ext. 488. Members: FREE Springfield residents: 
 $4 Nonmembers: $4 To attend via ZOOM\, please register in advance. Online 
 Registration (opens in new tab)\n\nSources: Explore Western Mass\nEvent pa
 ge: https://explorewesternmass.com/event/springfield-museums-a-la-carte-le
 ctures/2026-11-19/
LOCATION:Springfield Museums\, 21 Edwards Street\, Springfield\, MA\, 0110
 3
URL:https://explorewesternmass.com/event/springfield-museums-a-la-carte-le
 ctures/2026-11-19/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:“To the Farthest Ports of the Rich East” Lecture
DTSTART:20261119T183000
DTEND:20261119T200000
DTSTAMP:20261027T170709Z
UID:6c0a63e447bbfc7c9506eeab117836@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Join James Fichter as he discusses profit and peril in the new
  trade to Asia With independence from Britain secured\, in the 1780s Ameri
 can began trading to Asia for themselves. They bought tea in China. They s
 ailed to the East India Company-administered India for cloth\, to Yemen an
 d Dutch Java for coffee\, to Sumatra for pepper\, and to many other ports 
 in between. The trade to Asia accelerated as the French Revolutionary and 
 Napoleonic Wars (1793-1815) kept European traders out of Asian waters\, cr
 eating a tussle between between British and American crews to supply Frenc
 h-occupied Europe with Asian goods. This process created a new class of Am
 erican investor-capitalists and the first American millionaires. Yet such 
 wealth came at a cost\, as the ever growing and increasingly wealthy Ameri
 can trading fleet was a tempting target for foreign states and pirates—f
 rom Britain and France to Barbary and Yemen—against which the U.S. Navy 
 was not always a match.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://n
 orthofboston.org/event/to-the-farthest-ports-of-the-rich-east-lecture/
LOCATION:Salem Armory Visitor Center\, 2 New Liberty Street\, Salem\, MA\,
  01970
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/to-the-farthest-ports-of-the-rich-east
 -lecture/
END:VEVENT
